; What MAC-LANG-18's ordering costs, made loud (2026-08-16).
;
; Rules are tried IN ORDER and the first whose pattern matches wins,
; so a rule that nothing can reach is a program someone wrote
; expecting it to run. Which rules those are changed with MAC-LANG-15:
; the refusal used to be "two rules of one ARITY", whose stated reason
; - arity is what selects, so the second is unreachable - stopped
; being true the moment a pattern could fail. Two rules of one arity
; and different SHAPES are both reachable, and `392-macro-patterns.ax`
; is three of them.
;
; What survives is the part that was always the real claim: a rule
; whose every element is a plain BINDER matches every invocation of
; its arity, so nothing of that arity after it can ever run. That is
; irrefutability, it is decidable from the patterns alone, and it
; refuses exactly what the old rule refused among all-binder rules.
;
; The diagnostic names both rules by POSITION rather than by shape,
; because two rules of one arity may spell their patterns alike and a
; reader given only a shape would not know which line to delete.
(macro defU ((defU T)
   (:: (syntax/join u T) Int)
   (fn ((syntax/join u T)) 1))
  ((defU (a b))
   (:: (syntax/join u a) Int)
   (fn ((syntax/join u a)) 2)))

(:: main Int)

(fn (main) 0)
